<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyrrho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 360–c. 270 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pyrrho of Elis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pyrrho of Elis was a Greek philosopher regarded as the founder of scepticism. He travelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s army as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where he reportedly met ascetic sages. Teaching that the nature of things cannot be known, he recommended suspending judgement to reach tranquillity. He wrote nothing; his ideas survive through his pupil Timon and the later Pyrrhonist school.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyrrho.jpg" alt="Pyrrho" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Girolamo Olgiati · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyrrho_Heliensis_-_Illustrium_philosophorum_et_sapientum_effigies_ab_eorum_numistatibus_extractae.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pyrrho came from Elis, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a>. He was likely a member of the Klytidiai, a clan of seers in Elis who interpreted the oracles of the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a>, where Pyrrho served as a high priest. The Klytidiai were descendants of Klytios, the son of Alcmaeon and the grandson of Amphiaraus. In a poem called the Python, his pupil Timon of Phlius describes first meeting him on the grounds of an Amphiareion, a temple of Amphiaraus, while both were on a pilgrimage to Delphi.</p><p>Most biographical information comes from Diogenes Laertius, who drew chiefly on the mid-third-century BCE biographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> of Carystus. Unlike the founders of other Hellenistic philosophies, Pyrrho was not substantively influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>. He travelled with Anaxarchus on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s Indian campaign, which Diogenes dates to 327 to 325 BCE, and is said to have gone as far as the gymnosophists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. How much he took from this journey is disputed. Richard Bett heavily discounts Indian influence, noting that conversation with the gymnosophists needed three translators, while Johannes Bronkhorst sees no link between early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and Pyrrho's thought. It has also been suggested that the gymnosophists were Jains or Ajnanins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Pyrrho wrote nothing. Most of what is known of his philosophy comes from Timon, whose fragments were preserved mostly by Sextus Empiricus, Diogenes Laertius and Eusebius. Much of what is now called Pyrrhonism comes from the Outlines of Pyrrhonism by Sextus Empiricus, written over 400 years after Pyrrho's death, so little is certain about how his own views differed from the later school.</p><p>Most sources agree that his aim was ataraxia, freedom from mental disturbance, reached by giving up beliefs about thoughts and perceptions. A summary preserved by Eusebius, quoting Aristocles, quoting Timon, holds that things are equally indifferent, unstable and indeterminate, so that neither senses nor opinions are true or false and one should be without opinions, saying of each thing that it no more is than is not. Interpretations of this passage conflict. Many suggest Pyrrho held reality to be inherently indeterminate, which Sextus would have counted as a negative dogmatic belief.</p><p>It is uncertain whether Pyrrhonism continued without a break or died out and was revived; several centuries after Pyrrho, Aenesidemus led a revival. It was one of two major schools of scepticism of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>, the other being Academic scepticism. Pyrrhonists treat their philosophy as a way of life with Pyrrho as its model, seeking ataraxia through epoché, the suspension of judgement, by setting arguments of equal strength against each other.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pyrrho” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Elis produced the sophist Hippias and the sceptic philosopher <b>Pyrrho</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/elis">Elis</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Elis, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Elis, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.89,21.38&amp;year=-302" aria-label="Elis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pyrrho"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4420 4305h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4420 4305h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.89,21.38&amp;year=-302">What was here in 302 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
